   including the call and return.

   Original algorithm (forward substitution for L * x = b):

     for (i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
       x[i] = b[i];
       for (j = 0; j < i; ++j)
         x[i] -= L[i][j] * x[j];
       x[i] = x[i] / L[i][i];
     }

   Optimized algorithm (algebraically equivalent):

     For each row i, compute the dot product
       s = sum_{j=0}^{i-1} L[i][j] * x[j]
     using a scalar accumulator held in registers and a manually
     unrolled loop. Then:
       x[i] = (b[i] - s) / L[i][i];

   This preserves the exact computation while:
     - reducing memory traffic on x[i] (kept in a register),
     - providing a clear reduction pattern for the compiler,
     - improving ILP via unrolling, which helps SIMD/vectorization.
*/
static
void kernel_trisolv[[gnu::flatten, gnu::noinline]](int n,
		    DATA_TYPE POLYBENCH_2D(L,N,N,n,n),
		    DATA_TYPE POLYBENCH_1D(x,N,n),
		    DATA_TYPE POLYBENCH_1D(b,N,n))
